\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The cited spectral exponent}
\date{2026-11-07}

\p{This records \citet{Theorem 4.6}{kigami1996kokyuroku}
under the conventions printed in that section. It is not a
statement about an arbitrary sequence of graphs.}

\p{Let #{(K,S,\{F_i\})} be a post-critically finite
self-similar structure, and let #{(H_0,r)} be a regular
harmonic structure. The exposition normalizes the
compatibility constant to #{1}, and regularity then gives
#{0<r_i<1}. The initial operator is a resistance network
in the sense of \citet{Definition 2.1}{kigami1996kokyuroku}:
its energy is nonnegative and vanishes only on constant
functions. \citet{Proposition 2.2}{kigami1996kokyuroku}
characterizes such a network by symmetry, irreducibility,
a negative diagonal, zero row sums, and nonnegative
off-diagonal entries. Let #{\mu} be a Bernoulli measure,
#{(\mu_i)} positive and summing to #{1}, as defined after
\citet{Theorem 3.9}{kigami1996kokyuroku}, with positive
measure on every nonempty open set and no finite atoms.
The measured Laplacian is
\citet{Definition 3.10}{kigami1996kokyuroku}. By
\citet{Definition 4.1}{kigami1996kokyuroku}, a Dirichlet
eigenvalue satisfies #{\Delta_\mu u=-ku} with #{u=0} on
#{V_0}, and a Neumann eigenvalue satisfies the same
equation with normal derivative #{(du)_p=0} at every
boundary point. \citet{Definition 4.2}{kigami1996kokyuroku}
counts either spectrum with multiplicity.}

\p{Set #{\gamma_i=\sqrt{r_i\mu_i}}. Let #{d_s} be the unique
real solution of #{\sum_i\gamma_i^{d_s}=1}, equivalently
#{\sum_i(r_i\mu_i)^{d_s/2}=1}. The cited statement gives,
for both boundary conditions,
##{0<\liminf_{x\to\infty}\rho_*(x,\mu)/x^{d_s/2}\le\limsup_{x\to\infty}\rho_*(x,\mu)/x^{d_s/2}<\infty.}
If #{\sum_i\mathbb{Z}\log\gamma_i} is dense in
#{\mathbb{R}}, the limit exists. If that subgroup is
discrete with generator #{T>0}, then
##{\rho_*(x,\mu)=\bigl(G(\log x/2)+o(1)\bigr)\,x^{d_s/2},}
where #{G} is right-continuous and #{T}-periodic, with
#{0<\inf G\le\sup G<\infty}. The exposition says that
convergence of the ratio is still unknown, because #{G}
might be constant. The lattice clause therefore gives
neither a constant prefactor nor a proof that the periodic
factor is nonconstant. The 1996 text attributes the
theorem to Theorem 2.4 of Kigami and Lapidus,
Communications in Mathematical Physics, volume 158 (1993).
That article was not opened. This counting exponent is
not a logarithmic derivative of a heat trace, and it is
not a Hausdorff dimension.
\citet{Theorem 4.12}{kigami1996kokyuroku} uses another
measure and states #{d_S(\nu)=2d_H/(d_H+1)} for the
resistance metric.}

\p{\ref{fdip-00EX}, \ref{fdip-00EZ}, and \ref{fdip-00EY}
meet these hypotheses on the gasket. The resistances
#{\frac{3}{5}} stay those of the harmonic structure.
\ref{fdip-00EV} allows every positive triple summing to
#{1}. Each such factor is strictly less than #{1}, so the
support and atom comparisons of \ref{fdip-00EY} apply to
every such triple, and the exponent solves the displayed
equation. Those resistances do not select the weights.
The running choice #{\mu_i=\frac{1}{3}} is
\ref{fdip-00F5}. \ref{fdip-00F6} identifies the Dirichlet
spectrum with the weak generator on #{F_0}. The source
Neumann condition is not that generator.}

\proof{
\p{The two equations agree by substituting
#{\gamma_i=\sqrt{r_i\mu_i}}. The bounds, the density
alternative, and the periodic factor are the cited
statement. Post-critical finiteness is \ref{fdip-00EX},
#{\frac{3}{5}<1} and the regular structure are
\ref{fdip-00EZ}, and positive measure on nonempty open
sets together with null finite sets are \ref{fdip-00EY}.
The same null and support comparisons apply when the
factors are any positive triple from \ref{fdip-00EV}.}
}
